STEP 1 — Apply RI-FIX-001 → RI-FIX-014
RI-FIX-001 — Canonical Principal

We introduce:

identity.principals

A principal represents anything capable of:

producing an event
performing an action
operating a worker
owning a resource
authenticating
authorizing
participating in network operations

Types:

human
agent
service
system
network_node
external

Conceptually:

identity.principals
├── principal_id UUID
├── principal_type
├── name
├── status
├── created_at
├── updated_at
└── metadata

Agents become a specialized identity:

principal
   │
   └── agent

This eliminates the previous ambiguity between:

agent_id
actor_id
observer_id
service_id
node_id

where appropriate.

RI-FIX-002 — Event Sessions

Add:

event.sessions

Relationship:

Principal
    │
    ▼
Session
    │
    ▼
Events

An event may belong to a session, but the session itself does not become part of semantic memory.

Important:

session_id → event.sessions
actor_id   → identity.principals

with:

ON DELETE RESTRICT
RI-FIX-003 — Immutable Events

Events become genuinely immutable.

We enforce this at two levels:

Application

The API provides:

event.append()

but no:

event.update()
event.delete()
Database

Triggers reject:

UPDATE event.events
DELETE FROM event.events

The only supported lifecycle is:

append
   ↓
immutable
   ↓
referenced
   ↓
archival/retention policy

Even administrative users cannot silently rewrite historical events.

RI-FIX-004 — Observation Actors

Observations now use:

observer_id
    ↓
identity.principals

rather than an unconstrained UUID.

This means:

Observation
    │
    └── Observer
          │
          └── Principal

is always resolvable.

RI-FIX-005 — Versioned Episode Summaries

An episode itself is derived from events.

Its summary is another derived artifact.

Therefore:

episode
   │
   ├── episode_events
   │
   └── episode_summaries

A summary contains:

summary_id
episode_id
summary_text
model_id
operation_id
version
created_at

This means MOS can answer:

Which model generated this summary?

and:

What evidence did the episode summary derive from?

RI-FIX-006 — Typed Provenance

This is a major correction.

We do not want arbitrary:

target_type
target_id

for authoritative provenance.

Instead:

provenance.evidence_events
provenance.evidence_observations
provenance.evidence_entities
provenance.evidence_assertions
provenance.evidence_facts

Now PostgreSQL can actually enforce:

evidence_fact.fact_id
        ↓
memory.facts.fact_id

rather than merely trusting application code.

This gives MOS:

Relationally enforceable provenance.

RI-FIX-007 — Fact Version History

A fact is no longer simply:

fact.version = 4

Instead:

memory.facts
        │
        ▼
memory.fact_versions

Example:

Fact
  id = F123
  current_version = 3


Versions:


v1
User owns Framework Laptop


v2
User owns Framework Laptop
confidence = 0.98


v3
User no longer owns Framework Laptop
valid_until = ...

Nothing disappears.

This is essential for temporal reasoning.

RI-FIX-008 — Working Memory Uniqueness

Add:

UNIQUE(
    working_memory_id,
    object_type,
    object_id
)

Therefore:

working memory
       │
       ├── fact A
       ├── entity B
       └── episode C

cannot accidentally contain duplicate references.

RI-FIX-009 — Procedure Versioning

Procedures become versioned knowledge.

procedure
    │
    └── procedure_versions
             │
             └── procedure_steps

Example:

Deploy Redis


v1 → 8 steps → 72% success
v2 → 7 steps → 91% success
v3 → 7 steps → 96% success

The old versions remain available.

That allows MOS to learn rather than merely overwrite.

RI-FIX-010 — Task Dependency Integrity

Prevent:

Task A → depends on → Task A

with:

CHECK (task_id <> dependency_task_id)

More importantly, the planner must prevent cycles:

A → B
B → C
C → A

The database constraint alone cannot adequately solve arbitrary graph cycles.

Therefore:

database
    ↓
basic integrity


planner
    ↓
cycle detection
RI-FIX-011 — Execution → Event

Execution is now explicitly connected to the immutable event stream.

execution
    │
    ▼
actions
    │
    ▼
tool_calls
    │
    ▼
events

An execution should be able to answer:

What happened?
Who did it?
Why?
Which tool?
What parameters?
What result?
What changed?

This gives us the audit loop:

Plan
 ↓
Execution
 ↓
Action
 ↓
Tool Call
 ↓
Event
 ↓
Observation
 ↓
Knowledge
RI-FIX-012 — Graph Projection Ownership

Graph becomes explicitly a projection.

graph.projections
        │
        ├── graph.nodes
        │
        └── graph.edges

Both nodes and edges belong to a projection.

Therefore:

Projection V17
 ├── Node A
 ├── Node B
 └── Edge A→B

cannot accidentally combine with:

Projection V12

This becomes particularly important when we eventually have distributed graph rebuilds.

RI-FIX-013 — Version-Aware Cache

Semantic cache entries must know what they were based on.

Conceptually:

cache.entry
 ├── query_hash
 ├── result
 ├── knowledge_version
 ├── retrieval_policy_version
 ├── model_version
 └── created_at

Therefore:

knowledge changes
       ↓
cache becomes stale

rather than serving an old answer indefinitely.

RI-FIX-014 — Network Delivery Idempotency

Network delivery becomes:

event
  ↓
delivery
  ↓
target node

with:

UNIQUE(event_id, target_node_id)

MOS therefore assumes:

Network delivery is at-least-once.

Duplicate delivery must be harmless.

This is fundamental for a distributed MOS.

