STEP 3 — Lifecycle / State-Machine Validation

This is the next critical layer.

A database can have perfect foreign keys and still allow nonsensical state transitions.

So we define lifecycle state machines.

Event lifecycle

Events don't really have a mutable lifecycle.

APPENDED
   │
   ▼
IMMUTABLE
   │
   ▼
ARCHIVED

No:

UPDATE
DELETE
Entity lifecycle
ACTIVE
  │
  ├──► ARCHIVED
  │
  └──► MERGED
           │
           ▼
       SUPERSEDED

Importantly:

MERGE does not delete the original entity.

It creates lineage.

Entity A
   │
   └── merged_into → Entity B
Fact lifecycle

Facts require more sophisticated semantics.

PROPOSED
   │
   ▼
ACTIVE
   │
   ├──► SUPERSEDED
   │
   ├──► EXPIRED
   │
   ├──► REJECTED
   │
   └──► DISPUTED

A disputed fact isn't necessarily false.

It means:

MOS currently has conflicting evidence.

This distinction matters enormously.

Fact temporal model

We separate:

system time

from:

world time

Example:

Learned:
2026-08-13


Valid from:
2025-01-01


Valid until:
2026-06-01

MOS learned the fact today about something that happened in the past.

Therefore:

created_at

must never be confused with:

valid_from
valid_until

This should be a formal MOS invariant.

Procedure lifecycle
DRAFT
  │
  ▼
EXPERIMENTAL
  │
  ▼
VALIDATED
  │
  ▼
ACTIVE
  │
  ├──► DEPRECATED
  │
  └──► SUPERSEDED

A new learned procedure doesn't silently replace an old one.

Goal lifecycle
PROPOSED
   │
   ▼
ACTIVE
   │
   ├──► COMPLETED
   ├──► FAILED
   ├──► CANCELLED
   └──► ABANDONED
Task lifecycle
PENDING
  │
  ▼
READY
  │
  ▼
RUNNING
  │
  ├──► SUCCEEDED
  ├──► FAILED
  ├──► CANCELLED
  └──► BLOCKED

A task cannot become READY while dependencies are unresolved.

That becomes a planning invariant.

Execution lifecycle
CREATED
  │
  ▼
STARTED
  │
  ├──► SUCCEEDED
  ├──► FAILED
  ├──► CANCELLED
  └──► TIMEOUT
Network delivery lifecycle
QUEUED
  │
  ▼
SENT
  │
  ├──► ACKNOWLEDGED
  ├──► RETRYING
  ├──► FAILED
  └──► DEAD_LETTER

And retries must not create semantic duplicates.

STEP 3 RESULT

The lifecycle model is now internally coherent.

We have explicitly separated:

identity
existence
validity
version
provenance
status

This is important because those are not the same thing.

STEP 4 — Cross-Spec Validation

Now we check whether the specifications agree.

Core → Storage

Core says:

Every memory has identity, provenance, confidence and lifecycle.

Storage provides:

UUID
provenance
confidence
status
timestamps
versions

PASS

Core → API

Core operations:

remember
recall
search
learn
reflect
plan
update_fact
retrieve_context

API can map these to the underlying storage model.

But we make one important clarification:

remember()

doesn't directly create arbitrary facts.

Instead:

remember()
   ↓
event
   ↓
observation
   ↓
extraction
   ↓
candidate knowledge

This prevents API clients from bypassing provenance.

API → Storage

API must never expose unrestricted CRUD over authoritative objects.

Instead of:

DELETE /facts/:id

we want:

POST /facts/:id/supersede
POST /facts/:id/dispute
POST /facts/:id/archive

This respects the MOS lifecycle model.

PASS after API semantic clarification.

Worker → Storage

Workers should operate through controlled contracts.

For example:

Entity Extraction Worker


INPUT
event_id


OUTPUT
entity candidates
confidence
evidence
operation_id

Not:

UPDATE memory.entities ...

directly.

Workers produce operations, and operations modify authoritative state through controlled pathways.

This is a major architectural rule.

Worker → Event model

Every significant worker action should itself be observable.

Worker
 ↓
Operation
 ↓
Event

Therefore we can reconstruct:

Why did this fact appear?

Answer:

Fact
 ↓
Operation #891
 ↓
Fact extraction worker
 ↓
Event #38291
 ↓
Source

PASS

Network → Event model

Network is transporting MOS events, not arbitrary database mutations.

MOS Node A
    │
    │ Event
    ▼
MOS Network
    │
    ▼
MOS Node B

This is compatible with immutable events and idempotent delivery.

PASS

Graph → Knowledge

Graph is a projection.

Facts
  ↓
Graph

never:

Graph
  ↓
Truth

PASS

Embedding → Knowledge

Same:

Knowledge
   ↓
Embedding

not:

Embedding
   ↓
Knowledge

PASS

Attention & Reasoning Gateway

The gateway consumes:

working memory
facts
entities
relationships
episodes
procedures
plans
retrieval results

and returns:

reasoning operation

It doesn't become the source of truth.

PASS

